00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:06.610 align:start position:0%
many animals possess a magnificent  ability to control their appearance  especially cephalopods like the octopus  seen here by changing the color contrast  and texture of their skin these animals  create dynamic patterns on their bodies  to both blend in and stand out in their  environments the color-changing  capabilities of these wondrous creatures  have been the subject of fascination for  many researchers but have not yet been  replicated in synthetic systems I'm dr.  Stephen Morin a material scientist  working in the rapidly developing field  of soft machines in which we fabricate  synthetic systems using soft polymers  and flexible reinforcing sheets my  colleagues and I inspired by  color-changing cephalopods have created  soft robots with dynamic coloration and  contrast our approach combines  microfluidics pattern and color to not  only provide robots with camouflage or  display properties but also to propel  them colors and patterns are manipulated  by pumping colored or temperature  controlled fluids through a network of  microfluidic channels embedded in a thin  flexible silicon sheet called the color  layer the motion of these quadrupedal  Walker's is driven by the pneumatic  pressurization and inflation of micro  channels embedded in elastic polymers a  typical soft quadrupeds can move at  close to 40 metres per hour the color  layers are light and flexible enough to  be compatible with these stretching  machines without hampering their  movement color layers can be patterned  in many ways in this example the color  layer was given large disjointed patches  of contrasting colors to match its  variable environment  but camouflage is only one application  of color control mechanisms that can  make robots stand out against their  backgrounds are also useful for example  bright and contrasting colors can aid in  the location and recovery of machines  from poorly lit environments here you  can see a demonstration of fluorescent  and chemiluminescent solutions  camouflage and display can also be  manipulated in the infrared spectrum  through the use of temperature  controlled colorants in this  demonstration a robot is being displayed  in both the visible and infrared  spectrum the ability to control both  visible and infrared signatures is rare  in nature due to the limitations that  most organisms have in controlling their  own body temperatures these systems  provide the first steps toward imitating  the dynamic and complex color changing  functions of cephalopods they are  accessible and could be useful in many  disciplines such as the study of  camouflage use in nature and to  realistically simulate fluid vessels and  muscle motion
 

